  "result": "The gate now returns **OK** for `NRS_484B_413_2`: 7 witnesses, 2 of the 6 allowed runs used.\n\n**What the rule says**\n- **Who it applies to:** ego must be a vehicle (`ru_aggregate` with `DEF-NRS-484A-320::vehicle`).\n- **When it applies:** at the moment a left or right turn is recognized (`drv_motion_left_turn_from_onset` / `drv_motion_right_turn_from_onset`).\n- **What it requires:** at the turn's onset, the signal for that side must have been shown at every observation of the window. The onset is the last moment ego's turning state switched on. The signal is the turn indicator on that side or an arm signal meaning that side. The window is the last 100 ft travelled where the business-or-residence-district assumption holds, and the last 300 ft where it doesn't. Nothing after the onset is read.\n\n**One choice I made:** I wrote the district condition as \"signal over the last 100 ft, and either the district assumption holds or signal over the last 300 ft.\" The 300 ft window always contains the 100 ft one, so this matches the blueprint exactly. When the assumption is undeclared, the result is unknown only if the two windows disagree. The more literal if/then form would also be unknown when both windows fail, which the blueprint doesn't ask for. Since this follows the blueprint's own reading, I declared no narrowing.\n\n**Gate runs**\n1. **WITNESS_FAIL:** the gate rejected the traces because ego's heading stayed east while its yaw rate said it was turning. I rebuilt the turn so the heading rotates by an exact rational step of about 2° every 50 ms, with the matching yaw rate (±40.203 deg/s) and positions that follow the heading. The rule did not change.\n2. **OK.**\n\nI added one witness beyond the six planned: `branch_3`, a right turn with the indicator off and an arm signal meaning right, expected compliant. It tests the right-hand arm signal.\n\nI also made one mistaken shell call (an empty `python` command). It was denied and did nothing.\n\nFiles are in the working directory:\n- task_result.md",
